WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.845
Food travels through one long tube, called the alimentary canal. It runs from the mouth to the anus. In order: mouth, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, and out through the anus.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:24.960
Three more organs join the tube by small tubes called ducts: the liver, the gall bladder and the pancreas. Food never passes through them. They send juices into the gut.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:44.959
Now the jobs. Digestion means breaking large food molecules into small ones. In the mouth, teeth chew food into small pieces. Saliva is mixed in. Saliva has amylase, an enzyme: a protein that speeds up digestion. It starts to digest starch into maltose.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:57.817
The oesophagus carries food down to the stomach. The stomach makes hydrochloric acid, so its pH is about two. It also makes pepsin, a protease. Protein digestion starts here.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:13.355
The pancreas sends pancreatic juice into the first part of the small intestine, the duodenum. The juice has protease, amylase and lipase. The liver makes bile. The gall bladder stores it, and it also flows into the duodenum.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:26.025
The small intestine finishes digestion. Then it absorbs the small food molecules into the blood, through tiny folds called villi. It is the long, narrow, coiled tube in the middle.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:43.112
The large intestine is wider, and runs round the outside of the coils. It absorbs most of the water that is left. Without it, a person loses too much water and becomes dehydrated: the body has too little water. The faeces leave through the anus.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:55.215
Now a real exam question. The diagram shows the digestive system, with four labels: W, X, Y and Z. Name the four structures. Four marks.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:07.277
Start at the mouth and follow the tube down. X is the narrow tube going down through the chest: the oesophagus. Y ends on the bag that the oesophagus goes into: the stomach.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:21.845
Z ends on the many tight coils in the middle: the small intestine. W ends on the wider tube round the outside: the large intestine. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:32.806
A common mistake is to call the oesophagus the trachea, or the windpipe. The trachea carries air to the lungs. The oesophagus carries food to the stomach.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:44.579
Another mistake is to swap the two intestines. The small intestine is long and narrow, coiled in the middle. The large intestine is wider, and runs round the outside.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:03:03.343
Your turn. A doctor passes a thin camera tube in through the mouth, down to the duodenum. Name, in order, the organs it passes through. Then, a drug stops pancreatic juice reaching the duodenum. Which food is digested least, and why? Pause here and work it out.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:11.682
Part a: from the mouth, the tube goes down the oesophagus. Next it goes into the stomach, and then into the duodenum.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:24.943
Part b: lipids, which are fats and oils. Lipase comes only from the pancreas. Starch and protein are still partly digested, by amylase in saliva and by pepsin in the stomach.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:35.804
So, every time: start at the mouth and follow the tube down. Follow each label line to its end. Then give the job, with the enzyme and product where you can.
